   190	
   191	| Id | Rule | Examples | Source |
   192	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
   193	| AG-SUM-I | SUM over integers, where a surface or projection offers it: exact total over the solution rows (bag of distinct bindings); E-INTEGER-OVERFLOW only when the final total is outside the integer range; partial sums never wrap. Engine-side sums are required only for projections that declare them (G5.agg.projection-sums); per-subject sums run in the kernel | [2^62, 2^62] = E-INTEGER-OVERFLOW; [2^62, 2^62, -2^62] = 4611686018427387904; [2^58, 2^58, 2^58] = 864691128455135232 | RULINGS Q8a, NUMERIC; compiler.ts SUM over DECIMAL(32,0) |
   194	| AG-SUM-F | SUM over number, where offered: the correctly rounded value of the exact sum (one rounding), independent of evaluation order | [1e16, 1.0, -1e16] = 1.0 | RULINGS Q14c |
   195	| AG-AVG-I | AVG over integers without explicit conversion: preflight E-INTEGER-AVG |  | RULINGS Q8b; compiler.ts:1397-1401 |
   196	| AG-AVG-CONV | AVG(toNumber(integer)) and AVG(number): binary64 of the exact sum divided by the count, rounded once |  | RULINGS Q8b |
   197	| AG-COUNT | COUNT over a variable counts distinct flattened values of that variable (core), so two entities with value 10 count 1 while SUM counts 20 |  | testkit 'datalog aggregates preserve duplicate rows and define empty and distinct results' |
   198	| AG-EMPTY-UNGROUPED | ungrouped aggregate over no rows returns one row: COUNT 0; SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX null |  | same testkit case |
   199	| AG-EMPTY-GROUPED | grouped aggregate over no rows returns no rows; an empty group never appears |  | wiki/problems/native-correlation.md (grouped empty SUM produces no row) |
   200	| AG-MINMAX-TIES | MIN/MAX return the extreme value; a witness query keeps every tied entity |  | wiki/problems/native-correlation.md (tied integer-max winners) |
   201	| AG-PACKET-COALESCE | packet tasks that state COALESCE(sum, 0) return 0 for an empty input; this is task-specific and recorded in the task recipe |  | packet workload 06 empty-currency |
   202	
   203	### 4.5 Cardinality-many order
   204	
   205	set semantics: equal values collapse to one element; elements ordered first by type family, then by value. Family order: boolean, integer, number, decimal, datetime, string, ref, json, blob. boolean: false < true; integer: numeric; number: numeric IEEE (-0.0 and +0.0 collapse, CMP-NEGZERO); decimal: exact numeric value (1.50 and 1.5 are one value); datetime: instant; string: Unicode codepoint sequence; ref: codepoint sequence of the target id; json: codepoint sequence of the canonical JSON text; blob: codepoint sequence of the content id. Family order per RULINGS Q14a; decimal sorts with integer and number by numeric value only inside its own family (decimal is ordered after number).
   206	
